{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-3\\le v\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-2v,\\,-v,\\,2u⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-2v)\\cdot (-3)+(-v)\\cdot (2)+2u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+6v-2v+2u \\\\  & =-7u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,-y,\\,2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-7u+4v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-7u+4v"] ["math" "-7u-8v"] ["math" "4u+7v"] ["math" "7u+8v"] ["math" "8u-7v"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7u+4v"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-v} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)v,\\,(u-v)u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨(u-v)v,\\,(u-v)u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)v\\cdot (-1)+(u-v)u\\cdot (1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-uv+{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}-uv+uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(u+v)^{2}"] ["math" "(u-v)^{2}"] ["math" "{u}^{2}+uv-{v}^{2}"] ["math" "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}"] ["math" "{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161359" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,v+2(v-u),\\,2u-v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v-u,\\,3v-2u,\\,2u-v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(v-u)\\cdot 1+(3v-2u)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u-3v+2u+2u-v \\\\  & =3u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,y+2z,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(3u-3v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2u+v"] ["math" "2u-3v"] ["math" "3u-3v"] ["math" "3v+u"] ["math" "u+2v"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3u-3v"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,2u-v,\\,u+(2u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,2u-v,\\,3u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u+v,\\,2u-v,\\,3u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-2)+(2u-v)\\cdot (2)+(3u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u-2v+4u-2v+3u-2v \\\\  & =5u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5u-6v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(5u-6v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2u+v"] ["math" "11uv"] ["math" "3uv"] ["math" "5u-6v"] ["math" "u+v"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5u-6v"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-(u-2v),\\,u+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,3v-u,\\,u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(3v-u)\\cdot 2+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u+6v-2u+u+v \\\\  & =-2u+7v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y-z,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2u+7v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2u+7v"] ["math" "-2u+v"] ["math" "2u+4v"] ["math" "4u+v"] ["math" "4v-u"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161441" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2u+7v"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\,xz+Q+\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=-{x}^{2}z,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,-{x}^{2}z,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,-2xz,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2xz & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨-2xz,\\,-1,\\,-{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2P\\,xz+Q+R\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-2P$ and $2R$"] ["text" "$2P$ and $-2R$"] ["text" "$2P$ and $R$"] ["text" "$P$ and $-2R$"] ["text" "$P$ and $R$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161453" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2P$ and $R$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{}-Q\\frac{∂g}{}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$∂x$ and $∂y$"] ["text" "$∂x$ and $∂z$"] ["text" "$∂y$ and $∂x$"] ["text" "$∂y$ and $∂z$"] ["text" "$∂z$ and $∂x$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$∂x$ and $∂y$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-P$ and $-Q$"] ["text" "$-P$ and $Q$"] ["text" "$P$ and $-Q$"] ["text" "$P$ and $Q$"] ["text" "$Q$ and $P$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-P$ and $-Q$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $y=2x{z}^{3}$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\,{z}^{3}+Q-\\,x{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=2x{z}^{3},$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,2x{z}^{3},z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,2{z}^{3},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,6x{z}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2{z}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6x{z}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨2{z}^{3},\\,-1,\\,6x{z}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2P\\,{z}^{3}+Q-6R\\,x{z}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-2P$ and $-R$"] ["text" "$-2P$ and $6R$"] ["text" "$-2R$ and $-2P$"] ["text" "$P$ and $-6R$"] ["text" "$P$ and $R$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-2P$ and $6R$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\,z+\\,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=2yz,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨2yz,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨2z,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨2y,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2z & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2y & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(y,z))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(\\,-2Q)\\,z+(\\,-2R)\\,y\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-2Q$ and $-2R$"] ["text" "$-2Q$ and $R$"] ["text" "$-Q$ and $-R$"] ["text" "$Q$ and $-2R$"] ["text" "$Q$ and $R$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-2Q$ and $-2R$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy))=\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨{{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(xy),\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})-({cos}^{2}⁡(xy))\\cdot (\\frac{x}{{cos}^{2}⁡(xy)})+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\pi -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xy(2x+y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy(2x+y)}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\,-y,\\,xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 2-(-y)\\cdot (1)+xy(2x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+\\frac{1}{2}+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\,y-x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot (2x)-(y-x)\\cdot (2y)+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2}+2xy+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[y-{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(1-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-(-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x-y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "e-\\frac{15}{8}"] ["math" "e-\\frac{19}{8}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161476" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-\\frac{19}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨{{1}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fcc6e5e5dace3db1f93bc869d01232d35269ed72008a5716d4c43f6c74c60799"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}"] ["math" "\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "75e39366280c7906190aa57a635f5875a51b0003febca80ed9add9a24504575e"} {"question" "e-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle z+2y, \\: 3y+z, \\: x -z\\right\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-3x+2y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/assets/3761b12939f74f1e7f4d01ca83ea2884c1296672e22128064b109f7c42bb4df6.png)\nWhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12383.json" "file_sha256" "0432e61837fed8f1206db122739b63de1d7d54702c47ae6d78559c37f9032551"} {"question" "e-12383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=-3u+2v} \\\\  & =⟨(-3u+2v)+2v,\\,3v+(-3u+2v),\\,u-(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-3u+4v)\\cdot (3)+(-3u+5v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+12v+6u-10v+4u-2v \\\\  & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}u\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12383.json" "file_sha256" "0432e61837fed8f1206db122739b63de1d7d54702c47ae6d78559c37f9032551"} {"question" "e-12387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \n\\Bigg( \n\\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} \n+ Q \n- \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z}\n\\Bigg) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf j$-component.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12387.json" "file_sha256" "023ff0544af3dcfde73910172096bbf14e10e2ae5d1542218868c21f068fad20"} {"question" "e-12387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x = - \\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z =   \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12387.json" "file_sha256" "023ff0544af3dcfde73910172096bbf14e10e2ae5d1542218868c21f068fad20"} {"question" "e-12357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x, y, z) = \\left\\langle -y, \\: x, \\: z+2 \\right\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12357.json" "file_sha256" "9e103f5789edec7b5530d4f9dbf9ae301a72b05d12e7724a7f14de320599e9dc"} {"question" "e-12357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2x)-(x)\\cdot (2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/example-12357.json" "file_sha256" "9e103f5789edec7b5530d4f9dbf9ae301a72b05d12e7724a7f14de320599e9dc"}] "usage" {"q-161189" [] "e-12383" [] "e-12357" [] "e-12387" []} "basis" 2262 "retractions" [[17592186198532 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x, y)$ for $(x, y) ∈ D$, the flux of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨P, Q, R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)(A\\frac{∂g}{∂x} + A\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186197959 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle z+2y, \\: 3y+z, \\: x -z\\right\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-3x+2y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v$, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n$AAAAAAA$\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186197959 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v$, our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u + 2v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0,-3⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u + 2v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(u, v)) &= ⟨z + 2y, 3y + z, x - z⟩ \\mid _{x = u, y = v, z =-3u + 2v} \\\\\n&= ⟨(-3u + 2v) + 2v, 3v + (-3u + 2v), u - (-3u + 2v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3u + 4v,-3u + 5v, 4u - 2v⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}) &= ⟨ - 3u + 4v,-3u + 5v, 4u - 2v⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= (-3u + 4v) \\cdot (3) + (-3u + 5v) \\cdot (-2) + (4u - 2v) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-9u + 12v + 6u - 10v + 4u - 2v \\\\\n&= u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)⟨z + 2y, 3y + z, x - z⟩ \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)F(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)udA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{1}udvdu\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}$."] [17592186197962 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D$, the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\Bigg(\\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\Bigg) \\: \\textrm{d}A$,\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive $\\mathbf j$-component.*"] [17592186197962 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z)$, it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} &= ⟨1, \\frac{∂h}{∂x}, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{z}^{′} &= ⟨0, \\frac{∂h}{∂z}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{z}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix}1 &= ⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},-1, \\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x =-\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z = \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)F(r(x, z)) \\cdot (r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{z}^{′})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)⟨P, Q, R⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - \\frac{∂h}{∂x}, 1,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} + Q - R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186197949 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$\\mathbf F(x, y, z) = \\left\\langle -y, \\: x, \\: z+2 \\right\\rangle$,\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$."] [17592186197949 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(-P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x, y, g(x, y)) &= ⟨ - y, x, z + 2⟩ \\mid _{z = x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= ⟨- y_(⏟)_(P), x_(⏟)_(Q), x^{2} + y^{2} + 2_(⏟)_(R)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)⟨ - y, x, z + 2⟩ \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(- (-y) \\cdot (2x) - (x) \\cdot (2y) + x^{2} + y^{2} + 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(2xy - 2xy + x^{2} + y^{2} + 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2)dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[x^{2}y + \\frac{y^{3}}{3} + 2y]_{0}^{1}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3} + 2)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + \\frac{7}{3})dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{7x}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194094 17592186194095 17592186194096 17592186194097 17592186194098] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194093 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194093 :answer-field/id] [17592186194093 :answer-field/key] [17592186194093 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
